VALUES
 ('REF-00953','journal_article','Blackler','Alethea','Blackler A, Brophy C, O''Reilly M, Chamorro-Koc M',2018,
  'Seating in aged care: Physical fit, independence and comfort','SAGE Open Medicine','6',NULL,NULL,NULL,'2050312117744925',NULL,
  NULL,'10.1177/2050312117744925','29326817','PMC5758957',NULL,'2026-07-25',3,'clinical','AU',
  'EN','EN','VERIFIED','RESOLVED','mcp__PubMed__get_full_text_article','2026-07-25','high',0,
  'Staged in batch 1 as a hypothesis: possibly the only primary basis for the seat-dimension figures that otherwise rest on standards. CONFIRMED and stronger than expected — it does not merely supply dimensions, it demonstrates that a single seat height CANNOT serve the population, which is the Co-1 position batch 1 could only anchor on lived experience.',
  'R2 backward-mining lead from REF-00950 reference list; resolved via PubMed (Blackler seating aged care physical fit independence comfort)',
  'Full text retrieved and read via PubMed Central 2026-07-25. Tiered T3 not T1 on the authors own limitations statement: "The study was qualitative and collected subjective opinions of participants. It did not include objective measures of strain, pressure or movement." Audit + interview + video-observation, convenience sample, single Australian region.',
  'PRIMARY EVIDENCE FOR SEAT DIMENSIONS. n=19 aged care residents (6M/13F) + 5 staff + 2 experts (OT, physiotherapist), 4 facilities, Queensland AU; chair dimension audit (2 facilities + 4 websites, 54 seats); video-recorded sit-to-stand observation. || SEAT HEIGHT: "Residents, experts and staff all preferred higher seats to allow for effective STS transfers"; audited heights 410-505mm in facilities, 423-510mm on websites. BUT observed that "many residents could not touch the floor easily once they were seated". The paper names the conflict explicitly: "The disparity between seat height required for STS and seat height required for comfort while seated". RESOLUTION IS VARIETY, NOT A COMPROMISE NUMBER: "Variable height chairs, a range of chairs of different heights in each space and footrests could all address the height problem"; "providing a better variety of seat heights in each of the various contexts". Christenson (cited within) recommends a range of 380-457mm. || ARMRESTS: "When armrests were available they were used 100% of the time regardless of the resident''s level of mobility"; "Armrests were identified as vital by all participants"; "90% of those observed during STS needed more than one attempt to stand". Research cited within finds armrests ~250mm above seat best facilitate STS; participants reported SLIMMER armrests easier to grip, contradicting the 120mm width recommendation of Holden and Fernie. || SEAT DEPTH: observed 480-530mm in facilities vs mean buttock-popliteal length 440mm; excess depth forces slumping (posterior pelvic tilt). || SAMPLING CAVEAT recorded: "The researchers were not permitted by the facilities to approach potential resident participants directly" - facility-gatekept selection.',
  '2026-07-25','session_2026-07-25-energy-conservation-rest-points-seating-b2'),

 ('REF-00954','report','Transport Scotland',NULL,'Transport Scotland',NULL,
  'Inclusive Design in Town Centres and Busy Street Areas - Appendix C: Perspectives of disabled street users on inclusive physical design',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Appendix C',
  'Transport Scotland',NULL,NULL,NULL,'https://www.transport.gov.scot/publication/inclusive-design-in-town-centres-and-busy-street-areas/appendix-c-perspectives-of-disabled-street-users-on-inclusive-physical-design/','2026-07-25',5,'national_fw','GB-SCT',
  'EN','EN','VERIFIED',NULL,'WebFetch','2026-07-25','medium',0,
  'Staged in batch 1 as a hypothesis: expected to be a SECOND jurisdiction supplying an interval figure, letting 50m be compared against a Scottish number. WRONG - it states NO interval at all. That absence is more informative than a second number would have been.',
  'Transport for All disabled-led research street design seating rest points report (web)',
  'Retrieved 2026-07-25. National framework carrying a disabled-participant focus-group appendix; page states no publication date. Focus groups structured by impairment type (hearing impaired, visually impaired, deafblind, mobility impaired, learning/non-visible); participant count not stated for the seating discussions, recorded as a metadata gap rather than estimated.',
  'R15 CORRECTED FROM THE STAGED HYPOTHESIS. States NO seating interval and NO seat dimensions - only "at regular intervals where possible". A participatory national-framework appendix declining to specify a number is corroboration for GAP-304: the interval is unspecified even where disabled people were consulted directly. || NEW DEMAND-LAYER REQUIREMENT not present in REF-00949 or REF-00950: rest points must be VISIBLE AND ANTICIPATABLE IN ADVANCE. Verbatim: "Seating should, however, be positioned so that it is possible to see from a distance i.e. to allow journey planning" and "seating options needed to be clearly signposted for people who are unfamiliar with the area to allow them to know when to anticipate potential for breaks in their journey". An unseen bench does not support the decision to attempt the journey, so provision alone is insufficient. || Also: "It was noted that push up bars / handles on seating are also helpful in allowing older people and those needing to move between seats and wheelchairs to get up more easily" (converges with REF-00953 on armrests); "it was considered necessary to place seating well away from the main thoroughfare, and at regular intervals where possible".',
  '2026-07-25','session_2026-07-25-energy-conservation-rest-points-seating-b2'),

 ('REF-00955','journal_article','Nouri','Mohammadjavad','Nouri M, Chaudhury H',2025,
  'Individual and Neighborhood Characteristics of Walking Activity in People Living With Dementia: A Proof of Concept for Quantitative Spatial Analysis','Journal of Aging and Physical Activity','33','5','481','496',NULL,NULL,
  NULL,'10.1123/japa.2024-0121','40015287',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25',3,'clinical','CA',
  'EN','EN','VERIFIED','RESOLVED','mcp__PubMed__get_article_metadata','2026-07-25','high',0,
  'Sought as a GAP-306 independence test and expected at best another self-report cohort. Instead it measures walking by GPS, so the bench association is observed against RECORDED ROUTES rather than recalled barriers - a methodologically independent line, not just a geographically independent one.',
  'benches AND neighborhood AND older adults walking (pubmed)',
  'Metadata re-retrieved from PubMed 2026-07-25; DOI and PMID confirmed.',
  'GAP-306 INDEPENDENCE EVIDENCE (Canada). n=25 people living with dementia, Metro Vancouver; GPS-recorded regular walking routes + GIS spatial analysis. "higher number of benches and intersections, and a slight street network curvature showed positive associations with RWR length and walking time to regular destination within the 20-min walkshed." Objective route measurement, not self-reported barrier perception. Authors flag it as exploratory and limited by participant numbers.',
  '2026-07-25','session_2026-07-25-energy-conservation-rest-points-seating-b2'),

 ('REF-00956','journal_article','Maresova','Petra','Maresova P, Komarkova L, Horak J, Krejcar O, Kukuliac P, Vitkova L, Joukl M, Truhlarova Z, Tomsone S',2023,
  'Unveiling Seniors'' Perception of Mobility: Urbanization, Region, and Physical Activity','Patient Preference and Adherence','17',NULL,'3015','3031',NULL,NULL,
  NULL,'10.2147/PPA.S426789','38027077','PMC10675891',NULL,'2026-07-25',3,'clinical','CZ',
  'EN','EN','VERIFIED','RESOLVED','mcp__PubMed__get_article_metadata','2026-07-25','high',0,
  'Sought as a GAP-306 independence test. Did not expect a DISTRIBUTIONAL finding - that bench provision is itself unequally distributed rural vs urban - which reframes the gap as one of equity of provision, not only of adequacy.',
  'benches AND neighborhood AND older adults walking (pubmed)',
  'Metadata re-retrieved from PubMed 2026-07-25; DOI, PMID and PMCID confirmed.',
  'GAP-306 INDEPENDENCE EVIDENCE (Czech Republic; Latvian co-author). n=525, face-to-face and online questionnaire, ordinal regression. Treats benches as a named element of "urban scenes". Finding: "Substantially worse pedestrian conditions in availability of pedestrian crossings, benches, and lighting were recognized in rural municipalities versus cities." Bench provision is unequally distributed by degree of urbanisation - a distributional inequity distinct from the adequacy question.',
  '2026-07-25','session_2026-07-25-energy-conservation-rest-points-seating-b2'),

 ('REF-00957','journal_article','Amaya','Valkiria','Amaya V, Moulaert T, Gwiazdzinski L, Vuillerme N',2022,
  'Assessing and Qualifying Neighborhood Walkability for Older Adults: Construction and Initial Testing of a Multivariate Spatial Accessibility Model','International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health','19','3',NULL,NULL,'1808',NULL,
  NULL,'10.3390/ijerph19031808','35162830','PMC8834981',NULL,'2026-07-25',3,'clinical','FR',
  'EN','EN','VERIFIED','RESOLVED','mcp__PubMed__get_article_metadata','2026-07-25','high',0,
  'Sought as a GAP-306 independence test. Notable that public benches are one of only FOUR variables the model uses - a stronger status than "amenity" and consistent with the batch-1 position that rest provision is infrastructure, not decoration.',
  'benches AND neighborhood AND older adults walking (pubmed)',
  'Metadata re-retrieved from PubMed 2026-07-25; DOI, PMID and PMCID confirmed.',
  'GAP-306 INDEPENDENCE EVIDENCE (France, Grenoble conurbation). Multivariate spatial accessibility model built on FOUR independent variables, of which one is "public benches" (with pedestrian network; facilities and shops; slopes and gradients). Modelled across three older-adult profiles including "an older adult with reduced mobility". Exploratory model, two suburban neighbourhoods; no interval figure derived.',
  '2026-07-25','session_2026-07-25-energy-conservation-rest-points-seating-b2'),

 ('REF-00958','journal_article','Leung','Ka-Man','Leung KM, Ou KL, Chung PK, Thogersen-Ntoumani C',2021,
  'Older Adults'' Perceptions toward Walking: A Qualitative Study Using a Social-Ecological Model','International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health','18','14',NULL,NULL,'7686',NULL,
  NULL,'10.3390/ijerph18147686','34300136','PMC8303868',NULL,'2026-07-25',3,'clinical','HK',
  'EN','EN','VERIFIED','RESOLVED','mcp__PubMed__get_article_metadata','2026-07-25','high',0,
  'Sought as a GAP-306 independence test outside Europe and North America. Benches surface unprompted within a social-ecological framework, which is what a genuinely recurring demand should do.',
  'benches AND neighborhood AND older adults walking (pubmed)',
  'Metadata re-retrieved from PubMed 2026-07-25; DOI, PMID and PMCID confirmed.',
  'GAP-306 INDEPENDENCE EVIDENCE (Hong Kong). n=38 (19 women), mean age 72.8 (SD 7.4), semi-structured interviews, thematic coding in NVivo, social-ecological model. Benches named at the physical-environment level under "pedestrian facilities (benches, quality of walking paths and sidewalks, and aesthetics)". Qualitative; no interval figure.',
  '2026-07-25','session_2026-07-25-energy-conservation-rest-points-seating-b2'),

 ('REF-00959','journal_article','Sallis','James','Sallis JF, Cain KL, Conway TL, Gavand KA, Millstein RA, Geremia CM, Frank LD, Saelens BE, Glanz K, King AC',2015,
  'Is Your Neighborhood Designed to Support Physical Activity? A Brief Streetscape Audit Tool','Preventing Chronic Disease','12',NULL,NULL,NULL,'E141',NULL,
  NULL,'10.5888/pcd12.150098','26334713','PMC4561538',NULL,'2026-07-25',3,'clinical','US',
  'EN','EN','VERIFIED','RESOLVED','mcp__PubMed__get_article_metadata','2026-07-25','high',0,
  'Sought as a GAP-306 independence test. The strongest form the evidence could take short of an interval trial: benches SURVIVED empirical item-selection into a validated 15-item instrument, across the largest sample in the batch. Still supplies no spacing figure, which sharpens rather than closes GAP-304.',
  'benches AND neighborhood AND older adults walking (pubmed)',
  'Metadata re-retrieved from PubMed 2026-07-25; DOI, PMID and PMCID confirmed.',
  'GAP-306 INDEPENDENCE EVIDENCE (US, multicenter). n=3,677 across four age groups (758 children, 897 adolescents, 1,655 younger adults, 367 older adults); MAPS-Mini distilled from the 120-item Microscale Audit of Pedestrian Streetscapes, correlating r=.85 with the full instrument; accelerometer plus survey outcomes. Benches were RETAINED through item selection on the basis of association with physical activity: "Items related to active transport in 3 age groups were presence of sidewalks, curb cuts, street lights, benches, and buffer between street and sidewalk." Presence only - the instrument records whether benches exist, not their spacing.',
  '2026-07-25','session_2026-07-25-energy-conservation-rest-points-seating-b2');

